[' Here's a simple implementation of the `getValueFromArray` function in C, along with the main function to demonstrate its usage:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int getValueFromArray(int* arr, size_t size, size_t index) {
    if (index >= size) {
        return -1;
    }
    return arr[index];
}

int main() {
    int *arr = NULL;
    size_t arr_size;
    printf("Enter the array size: ");
    scanf("%zu", &arr_size);

    arr = (int*)malloc(arr_size * sizeof(int));

    for (size_t i = 0; i < arr_size; ++i) {
        printf("Enter element %zu: ", i);
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
    }

    size_t index;
    printf("Enter the index to retrieve value from array: ");
    scanf("%zu", &index);

    int result = getValueFromArray(arr, arr_size, index);
    printf("The value at index %zu is: %d\n", index, result);

    free(arr);
    return 0;
}
```

This code defines a `getValueFromArray` function that takes an integer array, its size and an index as parameters. It checks if the given index is within bounds of the array and returns the value at that index or -1 if it's out of bounds. The main function reads an array size, fills the array with user input, retrieves a value using `getValueFromArray`, and prints the result. Don't forget to compile this code using gcc or any other C compiler.

