[' Here's a C code implementation of the described function, called `scan`. The function uses a buffer of size 1023 to store the input string and dynamically allocates an additional byte for the null terminator.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

size_t scan(char* buffer, size_t bufferSize) {
    if (buffer == NULL || bufferSize <= 0) {
        return 0;
    }

    // Dynamically allocate additional byte for the null terminator
    char *tempBuffer = (char*)malloc(bufferSize + 1);
    if (tempBuffer == NULL) {
        return 0;
    }

    // Copy the original buffer to the newly allocated one, including the null terminator
    memcpy(tempBuffer, buffer, bufferSize);
    tempBuffer[bufferSize] = '\0';

    size_t charsRead = scanf("%1023[^\n]%*c", tempBuffer);
    if (charsRead <= 0) {
        free(tempBuffer);
        return 0;
    }

    // Copy the trimmed buffer back to the original one
    memcpy(buffer, tempBuffer, charsRead + 1);
    buffer[charsRead] = '\0';

    free(tempBuffer);
    return charsRead;
}
```

This implementation checks for valid input and returns the number of characters read in, excluding the newline character. The input string is stored in a dynamically allocated temporary buffer to ensure that it doesn't exceed the size of the original buffer, and the original buffer is copied back after trimming the newline character.']

